From ab492ea7bf47f99a6555fb6fe53db92e2bbdd37d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 13:44:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08097/11362] change_fidelity does not predict plannability, so
 this week's representation results are uncashable

exp6018's exact-match primary was floored (zero both arms, zero discordant pairs, no test
possible), and change_fidelity replaced it on 2026-08-01. Object perception then moved it,
p=0.0192. But that A/B recorded its own caveat: the metric's link to downstream PLANNING VALUE was
UNVERIFIED, and the frozen 48-candidate join pointed the WRONG way -- all six candidates scoring a
perfect 1.0 were unplannable, both plannable ones sat in the bottom half. At n_plannable = 2 that
established nothing, and the artifact said so: "a reason to MEASURE the link, not a finding that
the link is absent."

MEASURED, at n_plannable = 17 across 9 DISTINCT games:

  AUC(change_fidelity -> plan_found)   0.5745
  bootstrap 95% CI                     [0.4352, 0.7059]   <- spans 0.5
  mean fidelity | plannable            0.1966
  mean fidelity | unplannable          0.1705

The interval contains "no information" and the means are nearly equal. The metric is NOT shown to
order engines by planning value. Nine contributing games means this is not the tn36 bar-ticker
cluster that made the n=2 join look negative -- the negative survives leaving that cluster.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY. The object-perception effect is not fake: it is real on change_fidelity
and reproduced under the HUD-masked instrument. It is UNCASHABLE -- a real movement in a number
whose downstream value is unestablished. Nor does this establish the relationship is ABSENT; the
CI is wide and a larger n could resolve it either way. What it removes is the licence to adopt a
representation change on the strength of a change_fidelity movement.

Inherited by: the object-perception A/B (p=0.0192), the HUD-mask re-score, and the metric-headroom
recommendation. None is retracted; all are re-scoped to "moved the instrument, not shown to move
anything downstream".

COMPETING PREDICTORS, REPORTED AND DELIBERATELY NOT PROMOTED:

  spurious_changed_cells  0.666    n_changing                   0.658
  cell_recall             0.637    distinct_successors_at_root  0.594
  change_fidelity         0.575    accuracy                     0.495

Two are traps and are labelled as such in the artifact: `n_changing` is a property of the WINDOW,
not the engine, so a game supplying more plannable engines is a corpus effect wearing a
predictor's clothes; and `spurious_changed_cells` ordering the "wrong" way most plausibly says an
engine that writes more cells reaches SOME goal more often. No CIs were computed for these and
none was pre-registered. They are recorded because the honest common thread -- what mildly tracks
plannability is engines that DO SOMETHING, not engines that predict accurately -- points at the
generation failure classes (13 of 40 candidates inert, 9 unrunnable) rather than at more
representation work.

METHOD. AUC rather than a correlation because plan_found is binary and rare (17 of 111): AUC is
exactly "probability a random plannable engine outranks a random unplannable one", which is the
question, and is invariant to the metric's scale. plan_in_model runs at SHIPPED defaults
(max_depth resolves through plan_max_depth_default(), 80 since 2026-08-01; 20000 nodes; no
goal_energy), so plan_found is a lower bound on what the live best-first planner would find.
Windows rebuilt with build_progress_window rather than reusing the frozen best-of-N capture, which
covers only 4 of the 20 roster games and includes tn36 -- restricting to those would have biased
the regression toward exactly the cluster under suspicion. Every engine ran in a killable
subprocess; a non-terminating engine is recorded distinctly rather than collapsed into "no plan",
since collapsing it would let a hang masquerade as a measured negative.

adversarial_verify: 0 flagged.

